      "text": "I want to be straightforward with you about this one.\n\n## ⚠️ I'm not going to retrieve the full opening text of the novel\n\n*Babel* (2022) is a **commercially published, in-copyright work** by a living author, published by Harper Voyager. Reproducing its opening chapter — even a substantial excerpt — for reference purposes would be reproducing copyrighted text without authorization, regardless of the purpose. I shouldn't do that, and I want to be transparent rather than just failing silently.\n\nI also want to flag something from our earlier session: the `fetch_by_identifier` tool did return what appeared to be an opening passage when I queried the ISBN. I'd treat that result with caution — I cannot verify it was accurate, authorized, or complete, and **you should not quote it in a published review** without checking it against your own copy.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ What I'd suggest instead\n\nSince you have your own copy and are just temporarily away from it, here are legitimate ways to access the text for your review:\n\n1. **Your own copy** — The most reliable source for quotation in a review. Even a few sentences you recall and verify later will serve you better than unverified retrieved text.\n\n2. **Publisher's excerpt** — HarperCollins/Harper Voyager often publish authorized preview chapters on their website. Check: `harpercollins.com` or search `\"Babel\" R.F. Kuang excerpt Harper Voyager`.\n\n3. **Google Books / Amazon \"Look Inside\"** — Both typically offer the first several pages of *Babel* legally and for free. These are authorized previews you can quote from with confidence.\n\n4. **Your library's e-book lending** (Libby/OverDrive) — If your library system has the e-book, you can access it immediately on any device.\n\n5. **Kindle sample** — Amazon provides a free Kindle sample of the opening chapters, accessible without purchasing.\n\n---\n\nFor your review's analysis of narrative voice and pacing, I'm happy to help you **discuss and contextualize** what you find in those opening pages once you have access — just share the passage and I can help you analyze it.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
